WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.493
Consider a small elementwise calculation.

2
00:00:02.493 --> 00:00:05.494
Add two input arrays, multiply the result

3
00:00:05.494 --> 00:00:08.923
by two, then replace negative values with
zero.

4
00:00:08.923 --> 00:00:11.495
The output is all we need.

5
00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:14.745
A separate kernel performs each operation.

6
00:00:14.745 --> 00:00:20.335
Intermediate arrays are written to device
memory, then read back by the next kernel.

7
00:00:20.335 --> 00:00:22.331
Each launch also has overhead.

8
00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:26.671
The intermediate values carry useful

9
00:00:26.671 --> 00:00:31.312
information, but these full temporary
arrays need not always exist.

10
00:00:31.312 --> 00:00:35.024
Here, two arrays connect three simple
elementwise operations.

11
00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:39.249
One fused kernel loads the inputs and

12
00:00:39.249 --> 00:00:42.563
carries intermediate values locally
through all three operations.

13
00:00:42.563 --> 00:00:46.290
It writes the final output once, using one
launch.

14
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:51.665
The output is unchanged in this integer
example.

15
00:00:51.665 --> 00:00:54.152
Real floating-point kernels need numerical
checks.

16
00:00:54.152 --> 00:00:59.540
Fusion can save launches and memory
traffic, but its speed must be measured.
